<header class="hero sub">
  <div class="hbg" role="img" aria-label="A quiet Sunday morning on Rua de Santa Maria in Funchal's old town with painted doors" style="background-image:url('../assets/journal/quiet-sunday-morning-rua-de-santa-maria-funchal-madeira.jpg');background-image:image-set(url('../assets/journal/quiet-sunday-morning-rua-de-santa-maria-funchal-madeira.webp') type('image/webp'),url('../assets/journal/quiet-sunday-morning-rua-de-santa-maria-funchal-madeira.jpg') type('image/jpeg'))"></div>
  <div class="hov"></div>
  <div class="wrap hc">
    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Guide</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Can You Do in Funchal on a Sunday?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>27 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">More than people expect. The farmers&#x27; market and many small shops are shut, but the cable car to Monte, the Botanical Garden, the Monte Palace gardens, the big shopping centres and the old town&#x27;s cafés are open, and the sea does not take Sundays off.</p>

      <h2>What is closed in Funchal on a Sunday?</h2><p>Mainly the market and the small shops. Plan around these few closures and the rest of the day is easy.</p><ul><li><strong>Mercado dos Lavradores.</strong> Funchal's farmers' market, the big hall of fruit, flowers and fish, is closed all day on Sunday. It also closes early on Saturday, around 14:00, so a Saturday visit needs to be a morning one. There is more on opening times and what to buy in our <a href="/posts/mercado-dos-lavradores-funchal-guide">Mercado dos Lavradores guide</a>.</li><li><strong>Independent shops.</strong> Many of the small boutiques, delicatessens and craft shops in the centre close on Sundays. Shop for souvenirs on a weekday or on Saturday morning.</li><li><strong>Some museums.</strong> Several museums run by the regional government have kept Sundays closed in recent years. Check the one you want before you walk up the hill to it.</li><li><strong>Possibly the Monte toboggan.</strong> Sources disagree on Sunday opening, and the more recent ones say it does not run. Check with the carreiros before you plan the ride down.</li></ul><h2>What stays open on a Sunday?</h2><p>Most of what visitors come to Funchal for. These are the reliable ones:</p><ul><li><strong>The Funchal cable car</strong> keeps its normal service. Funchal's tourism listing gives a daily timetable of 9:00 until 17:45 for the line from the Zona Velha up to Monte, a quarter of an hour in the air over the roofs.</li><li><strong>Monte Palace Tropical Garden</strong>, at the top of the cable car, opens every day.</li><li><strong>The Madeira Botanical Garden</strong> keeps the same hours seven days a week, 9:00 until 18:00.</li><li><strong>The big shopping centres</strong>, such as Forum Madeira on the hotel side of town and La Vie in the centre, open on Sundays, with cafés inside.</li><li><strong>Cafés and restaurants</strong>, above all in the Zona Velha and along the seafront. Book a table for Sunday lunch if you have a particular place in mind.</li><li><strong>Wine lodges.</strong> Blandy's Wine Lodge lists Sunday hours on its own site, with a break at lunchtime, but other listings disagree. Check before you go.</li></ul><h2>Do boat trips run on Sundays?</h2><p>Yes. For a boat, Sunday is like any other day: what decides the trip is the sea, not the calendar. Chifbay's morning and afternoon Day Trips keep their 10:00 and 14:00 slots on Sundays. The Sunset Trip is timed from that evening's sunset, starting 75 minutes before it.</p><p>In fact Sunday is a good day to choose for the boat. With the market shut and the small shops closed, you lose less on land by spending three hours on the water. The <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip west to the coves</a> heads past Câmara de Lobos and under Cabo Girão to swim at Fajã dos Padres and Ribeira Brava. The <a href="/sunset-cruise">evening trip at sunset</a> turns at Cabo Girão after 2h or at Ribeira Brava after 2h30.</p><h2>What does a good Sunday in Funchal look like?</h2><p>Old town early, sea in the middle of the day, gardens in the afternoon. Here is one way to put it together:</p><ol><li><strong>Before 10:00, the Zona Velha.</strong> Walk Rua de Santa Maria while it is quiet. The painted doors are the point, and they are all outside, so it does not matter that the shops behind them are shut. Coffee on the way.</li><li><strong>10:00, the boat.</strong> Aim to stand on the marina pontoon 15 minutes ahead of departure. The marina sits halfway along the seafront, an easy walk from the old town. The 2h30 Day Trip has you back at 12:30, the 3h one at 13:00.</li><li><strong>Lunch, back in the old town.</strong> Salt in your hair, a long table, no hurry.</li><li><strong>Mid-afternoon, the cable car to Monte.</strong> Spend an hour or two in the Monte Palace gardens, then ride the cable car back down before the last car, or take a taxi.</li><li><strong>Evening, the seafront.</strong> Walk west along the promenade as the light drops, or choose the Sunset Trip instead of the morning boat and swap the order of the day.</li></ol><p>If you prefer a slow morning, flip it: gardens and Monte first, then the 14:00 Day Trip, back on the pontoon at 16:30 or 17:00.</p><h2>What should you move off a Sunday?</h2><p>Anything that depends on shops or the market. A quick rule of thumb:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Sunday?</th><th>Better day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mercado dos Lavradores</td><td>No, closed</td><td>Any weekday, or Saturday morning</td></tr><tr><td>Souvenir and craft shopping</td><td>Limited</td><td>Weekdays</td></tr><tr><td>Cable car and Monte Palace</td><td>Yes</td><td>Any day</td></tr><tr><td>Botanical Garden</td><td>Yes</td><td>Any day</td></tr><tr><td>Boat trip</td><td>Yes</td><td>Any day with a calm sea</td></tr><tr><td>Monte toboggan</td><td>Check</td><td>Monday to Saturday</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The table covers Funchal itself. A Sunday drive works well too, because the big viewpoints are open air. The Cabo Girão viewpoint, with its glass skywalk, keeps the same hours all week, eight in the morning to eight at night, according to Madeira's tourism board. Pico do Arieiro and Eira do Serrado are lookouts a short walk from the car park, so the only thing that can close them is cloud. If you would rather see the mountains than the market, Sunday is a good day to do it, and you are back in town for dinner.</p><h2>When is a Sunday in Funchal a poor plan?</h2><p>When your trip is short and the market or the shops were the reason you came. It depends on what you want from the city. If you have two days and one of them is a Sunday, put the market and the shopping on the other day, and give Sunday to the sea and the gardens.</p><p>Two other things are worth a look before you plan the weekend. Public holidays bring their own closures, much like a Sunday, and one that falls next to a weekend can make it a long quiet stretch. Our guide to <a href="/posts/madeira-public-holidays-guide">public holidays in Madeira</a> lists the dates. And if a boat is part of the plan, book it early in your stay rather than on your last Sunday, so a rough sea still leaves a day to move to. Cancelling a Chifbay trip is free up to 24 hours before departure.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can you visit Funchal's farmers' market on a Sunday?</h3><p>No, Funchal's farmers' market is closed on Sundays. It opens Monday to Saturday, and closes early on Saturday afternoon.</p><h3>Are the shops open in Funchal on Sundays?</h3><p>The big shopping centres, such as Forum Madeira and La Vie, open on Sundays. Many small independent shops in the centre close.</p><h3>Does the cable car to Monte run on Sundays?</h3><p>Yes. Funchal's tourism listing shows it running seven days a week, 9:00 until 17:45. The ride up takes around a quarter of an hour.</p><h3>Can you do a boat trip from Funchal on a Sunday?</h3><p>Yes. Both trips sail on Sundays, morning, afternoon and sunset, whenever the sea allows.</p>

      <blockquote>The market rests on Sunday. The sea does not.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

